RT64 port patch 0005: vsync pacing (Blast Corps port; MIT, as RT64).

With vsync on, RT64's present thread handed the swap chain a picture every
1 / targetRate s on its own timer, and plume's D3D12 swap chain presents with
a sync interval of 0 (present wait): the display shows whichever picture is
newest at each refresh.  At a target rate equal to the display's the timer
beat against the refreshes and the present thread's lag crept up until the
workload queue rendered one picture instead of two for some frames (measured
with DXGI frame statistics on a 60 Hz display: 6-18 % of the shown intervals
two refreshes instead of one at 60; in `original`, 16-40 % of the 30 fps
frames held 1 or 3 refreshes instead of 2).

With this patch (PresentQueue::holdFor, waitForHold, dropToCatchUp,
noteStruggle, swapChainPresent), with vsync on:
  - (D3D12 only: Vulkan's FIFO swap chain never drops a picture and keeps
    RT64's pacing) every picture is presented with a sync interval of 1:
    never dropped by the display; no timer;
  - the picture before stays for the refreshes the timeline gives it:
    in-between (interpolated) pictures 1 refresh at the display's rate, N at
    1/N of it (other target rates: RT64's timer, as before); a game picture
    the refreshes between its submission and the next one's, less its
    in-between pictures' (Present::submitTime: the host's retrace due time,
    State::presentDueTime, else the time it was queued).  A hold over one
    refresh waits for the middle of the refresh before the due one, counted
    from when the picture before was really shown (DXGI frame statistics), so
    the phase of the game's retraces against the display's doesn't matter;
    clock drift changes a hold only at 3/4 of a refresh;
  - latency: an in-between picture that would queue behind another and show
    a refresh later than the lowest such latency of the last seconds is
    dropped once; a wait that
    stayed a refresh too long for 30 pictures is shortened once;
  - the workload queue reduces the in-between pictures it renders
    (frameReduction) only when it waited over half a display frame for the
    present thread to let go of a target (with vsync pacing the present
    thread lets go of it on a refresh: any wait used to trigger it, making
    every third game frame one picture short);
  - when the PC can't keep up (six drops or presents over five refreshes
    after queueing within 2 s), RT64's own pacing for 5 s.
The host can turn it off (PresentQueue::vsyncPacing) and watch every present
(presentedHook).  RT64's interpolation weights and the game's timing are
untouched; without vsync pacing everything is as before.

